    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Following that logic, could you go into more detail about the later stages? Specifically, how do the Amadori rearrangement products eventually lead to the formation of the various melanoidins that give food its color and complex aroma? I'm trying to connect the molecular fragmentation to the sensory profiles we see in roasted meats.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The Amadori rearrangement products, which are formed after the initial glycosylamine intermediate undergoes further structural changes, are essentially the starting blocks for the final, complex flavor compounds. These intermediates can then undergo a series of fragmentation, dehydration, and polymerization reactions.\n\nThis fragmentation process is where the molecular complexity explodes, yielding hundreds of different volatile compounds responsible for the roasted meat aromas—think of those nutty, savory notes. Simultaneously, these fragments react with each other and with other molecules to form high-molecular-weight polymers known as melanoidins. These melanoidins are the large, brown, often dark pigments that give roasted foods their characteristic color.",
      "source": "target",
